# A small literate program

A complete example is the quickest way to see how explanation and source can share
one structure. Suppose we keep a checklist in Markdown and want a small program to
summarize it. Checked items begin with `[x]`, unchecked items with `[ ]`, and all
other lines are ignored.

Given this text:

```text
[x] Choose an example
[ ] Write the first draft
A sentence that is not a checklist item
[x] Draw the fragment graph
```

we want the report:

```text
2 complete, 1 remaining
```

Before thinking about programming syntax, we can divide the task into four ideas:
receive the checklist text, recognize checklist items, count their states, and
format a report.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Root[Summarize a checklist] --> Receive[Receive checklist text]
    Root --> Recognize[Recognize checklist items]
    Root --> Count[Count checklist states]
    Root --> Format[Format the checklist report]
```

The graph is small, but it already has the shape of a larger literate program. The
root describes the whole. Each child gives a name to one responsibility that can
be explained independently.

## Write the program as an outline

A root fragment associates the explanation with a generated file. Its body can
read like an outline:

```text
\<<summarize a checklist.*>>= checklist-summary.js $
\<<receive checklist text>>
\<<recognize checklist items>>
\<<count checklist states>>
\<<format the checklist report>>
```

The star in the root name marks a family that produces an output. The filename and
final dollar sign make the destination explicit. The four references can be
written before their definitions because the book, rather than a compiler's
single pass through this page, determines their relationship.

At this point a reader knows the program's broad structure without seeing a line
of JavaScript. That is useful information, not a temporary placeholder. The root
fragment remains a compact description even after every child is implemented.

## Receive the checklist text

For this small example, every command-line argument is one input line. Joining the
arguments gives the rest of the program one string to examine:

```text
\<<receive checklist text>>=
const input = process.argv.slice(2).join('\n');
```

A production command might read a file or standard input instead. That choice is
separate from understanding the checklist grammar, which is why it has its own
name.

## Recognize the input

A checklist marker has a small grammar. After optional indentation, it contains an
opening bracket, either a space or `x`, a closing bracket, and the item's text. We
can explain that rule before expressing it as a regular expression.

```text
\<<recognize checklist items>>=
function checklistState(line) {
    const match = /^\s*\[([ x])\]\s+/.exec(line);
    if (!match) return undefined;
    return match[1] === 'x' ? 'complete' : 'remaining';
}
```

The function deliberately returns no state for ordinary prose. That decision is
part of the model: ignoring unrelated lines is expected behavior, not an error to
be handled somewhere else.

## Count what was recognized

Let *C* be the number of complete items and *R* the number remaining. If the
input contains *N* recognized checklist items, then:

```text
N = C + R
```

This formula is simple, but it states a useful invariant. Every recognized item
must contribute to exactly one count. An item cannot disappear and cannot be
counted twice.

```text
\<<count checklist states>>=
const totals = { complete: 0, remaining: 0 };
for (const line of input.split(/\r\n|\r|\n/)) {
    const state = checklistState(line);
    if (state) totals[state]++;
}
```

The code illustrates the invariant rather than replacing its explanation. A reader
who skips the block still knows what is counted and what must remain true.

## Format the result

Presentation is the final responsibility. It does not need to know how a checklist
marker was recognized:

```text
\<<format the checklist report>>=
console.log(`${totals.complete} complete, ${totals.remaining} remaining`);
```

Separating recognition from presentation may look generous for such a small
program. Its value becomes clearer when either idea changes. Supporting uppercase
`X` belongs with recognition; translating the report belongs with formatting.
The names show where each decision fits.

## Continue an idea later

A fragment family may receive more than one contribution. Suppose a later section
introduces a command-line input file. The root need not be rewritten in place; the
idea that obtains input can be extended where that new concern is explained.

Contributions are not an excuse to scatter one idea arbitrarily. They are valuable
when the narrative genuinely returns to a responsibility with new knowledge. Their
order is deterministic, and the generated source receives each contribution in
that order.

## Tangle and weave

Tangling follows the four references and produces one ordinary source file:

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Book[Literate chapter] --> Graph[Resolve named fragments]
    Graph --> Program[checklist-summary.js]
    Book --> Weave[Render prose, diagrams, and fragments]
    Weave --> Page[Readable chapter]
```

The generated file contains no fragment markers. JavaScript tools can read it as
if it had been written directly. The woven page keeps the names, explanations,
and relationships because those are what help a person understand the program.

## Failure should preserve the last good result

If the root refers to `<<read checklist input>>` but no such fragment exists, the
graph is incomplete. Literate reports the missing name and produces no generation
plan. It does not replace a previously valid program with a partial file.

That behavior follows an important principle used throughout this book: inspect
and validate a complete plan before performing an external effect. Later chapters
apply the same principle to filesystem transactions, publication, language-server
edits, and PDF compilation.

This example introduced fragments by use. The next chapter asks how to choose them
well, because the notation alone cannot turn an arbitrary code split into a clear
explanation.
